# Biographies, antiquity to about 1750. Written from the retrieved Wikipedia lead of each person's article.
# Article | born..died | place | lenses | tags | Explorer | Kid | display name (optional)
The Buddha | 6th century BCE..5th century BCE | Maya Devi Temple, Lumbini | religion | saint, thinker | Siddhartha Gautama, known as the Buddha, was a wandering religious teacher who lived in the eastern Indo-Gangetic Plains in the 6th or 5th century BCE. According to Buddhist legends he was born at Lumbini, in what is now Nepal, to royal parents of the Shakya clan, and gave up his home life. | The Buddha was a teacher who lived about 2,500 years ago. Stories say he was born a prince at Lumbini, in today's Nepal, and gave up his rich life to search for the truth. Buddhism is the religion that follows his teaching.
Mahavira | 599 BCE | Bihar | religion | saint, thinker, reformer | Mahavira, born Vardhamana, was a religious reformer and spiritual leader, considered by Jains to be the 24th and final Tirthankara. By traditional accounts he was born in 599 BCE into a ruling family. | Mahavira was a great teacher of the Jain religion. Jains count him as the last of 24 great teachers. Tradition says he was born a prince in 599 BCE.
Ashoka | 304 BCE..232 BCE | Pataliputra | power, religion | ruler | Ashoka, known as Ashoka the Great, was Emperor of Magadha from about 268 BCE until his death in 232 BCE, and the third ruler of the Maurya Empire. | Ashoka was the third emperor of the Maurya family and ruled most of India. He had his messages carved on rocks and pillars across the land.
Chandragupta Maurya | 320 BCE..298 BCE | Pataliputra | power, war | ruler | Chandragupta Maurya, who reigned from about 320 to 298 BCE, was the founder and first emperor of the Maurya Empire, based in Magadha. | Chandragupta Maurya founded the Maurya Empire about 2,300 years ago and was its first emperor.
Chanakya | 4th century BCE | Pataliputra | power, science | thinker, politician | Chanakya, according to legendary narratives preserved from the 4th to the 11th century CE, was a Brahmin who assisted the first Mauryan emperor, Chandragupta. | Old stories say that a clever teacher named Chanakya helped Chandragupta Maurya to win his empire.
Pāṇini | 6th century BCE..4th century BCE | India | language, science | scientist, thinker | Panini was an ancient Sanskrit grammarian and linguist. His masterwork, the Ashtadhyayi, dated somewhere between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE, is the foundational text of the Indian science of grammar. | Panini was a scholar who worked out the rules of the Sanskrit language and wrote them down in a famous book. | Panini
Aryabhata | 476..550 | India | science | scientist | Aryabhata was the first of the major mathematician-astronomers of the classical age of Indian mathematics and astronomy. His works include the Aryabhatiya, which mentions that in 499 CE he was 23 years old. | Aryabhata was a great mathematician and astronomer. He wrote his famous book when he was only 23 years old.
Varāhamihira | 505..587 | Ujjain | science | scientist | Varahamihira was an ancient Indian mathematician and astronomer who lived in Ujjain, in the Avanti region of today's Madhya Pradesh. | Varahamihira was an astronomer who lived in the city of Ujjain about 1,500 years ago. | Varahamihira
Brahmagupta | 598..668 | Bhinmal | science | scientist | Brahmagupta, born at Bhillamala, today's Bhinmal in Rajasthan, was a mathematician and astronomer credited as the first person to understand and formalise the concept of zero as a number. His Brahmasphutasiddhanta is dated 628. | Brahmagupta was a mathematician born in Rajasthan. He was the first to write down rules for doing sums with zero.
Bhāskara II | 1114..1185 | India | science | scientist | Bhaskara II, known as Bhaskaracharya, was a polymath, mathematician and astronomer whose works represent the high point of the classical Siddhanta school. He was born in 1114. | Bhaskara was one of India's greatest mathematicians and astronomers. He was born in 1114. | Bhaskara II
Kalidasa | 4th century | India | arts, language | writer, poet | Kalidasa was a Classical Sanskrit author often considered ancient India's greatest poet and playwright. He is often placed in the 4th century CE, though his dates are uncertain. | Kalidasa wrote plays and poems in Sanskrit about 1,600 years ago. Many people think he was the greatest poet of ancient India.
Thiruvalluvar | 4th century BCE..5th century | Tamil Nadu | arts, language | poet, thinker | Thiruvalluvar was a philosopher and Tamil-language poet of the post-Sangam era, best known as the author of the Tirukkural, a collection of couplets. His lifetime has been dated variously from the fourth century BCE to the early fifth century CE. | Thiruvalluvar was a Tamil poet and thinker. He wrote the Kural, a famous book of very short poems about how to live well. Nobody is sure exactly when he lived.
Avvaiyar (Sangam poet) | 300 BCE..300 | Tamil Nadu | arts, language, women | woman, poet | Avvaiyar was a Tamil poet who lived during the Sangam period. She wrote 59 poems in the anthology Purananuru. | Avvaiyar was a Tamil woman poet who lived about 2,000 years ago. Fifty-nine of her poems survive in one old collection. | Avvaiyar
Kanishka | 127..150 | India | power, religion, trade | ruler | Kanishka I, known as Kanishka the Great, was an emperor of the Kushan dynasty, reigning about 127 to 150 CE. Under him the empire reached its zenith. | Kanishka was the greatest emperor of the Kushans. He ruled about 1,900 years ago.
Samudragupta | 318..375 | India | power, war | ruler | Samudragupta was the second emperor of the Gupta Empire, a son of the emperor Chandragupta I and the Licchavi princess Kumaradevi. | Samudragupta was the second emperor of the Gupta family. His mother was a princess of the Licchavi clan.
Xuanzang | 602-04-06..664-02-05 | India | religion, movement, language | traveller, saint | Xuanzang, born near present-day Luoyang in China on 6 April 602, was a 7th-century Chinese Buddhist monk, scholar, traveller and translator. | Xuanzang was a Buddhist monk, scholar and traveller from China who lived about 1,400 years ago.
Faxian | 337..422 | India | religion, movement | traveller, saint | Faxian was a Chinese Buddhist monk and translator who travelled on foot to classical India to acquire Buddhist texts. | Faxian was a Buddhist monk who walked all the way from China to India to collect holy books.
Nagarjuna | 150..250 | India | religion, science | thinker, saint | Nagarjuna, about 150 to 250 CE, was a scholar-monk, philosopher and teacher of Mahayana Buddhism from southern India. He is considered the founder of the Madhyamaka school. | Nagarjuna was a Buddhist monk and deep thinker from southern India who lived about 1,800 years ago.
Adi Shankara | 8th century | India | religion | thinker, saint | Adi Shankara was a Vedic scholar-monk, philosopher and teacher of Advaita Vedanta, who wrote influential commentaries. Scholars place him in the first half of the 8th century CE, though tradition gives a much earlier date. | Adi Shankara was a wandering monk and thinker who lived about 1,300 years ago. He taught a philosophy called Advaita Vedanta.
Andal | 8th century | Tamil Nadu | religion, arts, women | woman, saint, poet | Andal, also known as Kodhai, is one of the 12 Alvars, the Tamil poet-saints of the Bhakti movement, and the only woman among them. Active in the eighth century CE, she is credited with the Tamil works Tiruppavai and Nachiyar Tirumoli. | Andal was a Tamil poet and saint who lived about 1,300 years ago. She is the only woman among twelve famous poet-saints, and her songs are still sung.
Rajaraja I | 947-11-03..1014 | Thanjavur | power, war, arts | ruler, builder | Rajaraja I, known as Rajaraja the Great, was a Chola emperor who reigned from 985 to 1014, known for his conquests in southern India and Sri Lanka. | Rajaraja was a great Chola emperor. He conquered much of southern India and built the giant temple at Thanjavur.
Rajendra I | 971-07-26..1044 | Thanjavur | power, war, trade | ruler | Rajendra I, often called Rajendra the Great, was a Chola emperor who reigned from 1014 to 1044. He was born in Thanjavur, the son of Rajaraja I. | Rajendra was the son of Rajaraja and the next Chola emperor. His ships sailed as far as Southeast Asia.
Kharavela | 2nd century BCE..1st century BCE | Udayagiri and Khandagiri caves | power, religion | ruler | Kharavela was the emperor of Kalinga in the 2nd or 1st century BC. The main source for his life is his rock-cut Hathigumpha inscription, which is undated. | Kharavela was a king of Kalinga, in today's Odisha. Almost everything we know about him comes from one long message carved in a cave.
Pulakeshin II | 609..642 | Badami cave temples | power, war | ruler | Pulakeshin II was the Chalukya emperor from about 609 to 642. During his reign the Chalukya empire of Vatapi expanded to cover most of the Deccan. | Pulakeshin was a Chalukya king who ruled most of the Deccan about 1,400 years ago.
Bodhidharma | 5th century..6th century | India | religion, movement | saint, traveller | Bodhidharma was a semi-legendary Buddhist monk who lived during the 5th or 6th century CE. He is traditionally credited with bringing Chan Buddhism to China. | Bodhidharma was a Buddhist monk who, the stories say, travelled to China and began the kind of Buddhism known as Chan, or Zen.
Atiśa | 982..1054 | Vikramashila | religion, movement | saint, thinker | Atisha Dipamkara Shrijnana was a Buddhist religious teacher and leader from Bengal, generally associated with the works he wrote at the Vikramashila monastery. | Atisha was a Buddhist teacher from Bengal who lived about a thousand years ago. | Atisha
Ramanuja | 1077..1157 | India | religion | thinker, saint, reformer | Ramanuja was a Hindu philosopher, guru and social reformer, one of the most important exponents of the Sri Vaishnavism tradition. | Ramanuja was a Hindu teacher and thinker who lived about 900 years ago.
Akka Mahadevi | 1130..1160 | India | religion, arts, women | woman, saint, poet | Akka Mahadevi was a 12th-century philosopher, poet and mystic, and a prominent member of the Lingayat movement. Some 430 of her short poems, called vachanas, are known. | Akka Mahadevi was a poet and saint who lived about 850 years ago. She left about 430 short poems.
Lalleshwari | 1320..1392 | Kashmir Valley | religion, arts, women, language | woman, saint, poet | Lalleshwari, commonly known as Lal Ded, was a Kashmiri mystic of the Kashmir Shaivism school and the creator of a style of mystic poetry. | Lal Ded was a holy woman and poet of Kashmir who lived about 650 years ago. | Lal Ded
Kabir | 1398..1518 | Varanasi | religion, arts | saint, poet, reformer | Kabir, who flourished in the 15th century, was a devotional mystic poet and saint, born in Varanasi and known for being critical of organised religions. His verses are found in the Sikh scripture. Historians differ on his dates, some favouring 1398–1448 and others 1440–1518. | Kabir was a poet and saint from Varanasi who lived in the 1400s. He said that people argued too much about religion. His poems are still sung.
Ravidas | 15th century..16th century | Uttar Pradesh | religion, arts | saint, poet | Ravidas, or Raidas, was a mystic poet-saint of the Bhakti movement during the 15th to 16th century CE, venerated as a guru in several regions of northern India. | Ravidas was a poet and saint who lived about 500 years ago. Many people in northern India still honour him as their guru.
Mirabai | 16th century | Rajasthan | religion, arts, women | woman, saint, poet | Meera, better known as Mirabai, was a 16th-century Hindu mystic poet and devotee of Krishna. She was born into a Rathore Rajput royal family in today's Rajasthan and spent her childhood in Merta. | Mirabai was a princess of Rajasthan who lived about 500 years ago. She gave her life to singing songs of love for the god Krishna.
Chaitanya Mahaprabhu | 1486-02-18..1534-06-14 | West Bengal | religion | saint | Chaitanya Mahaprabhu, born Vishvambhara Mishra, was a Hindu saint from Bengal and the founder of Gaudiya Vaishnavism. | Chaitanya was a Hindu saint from Bengal who founded a new religious tradition.
Tulsidas | 1532..1623 | Awadh | religion, arts, language | poet, saint | Tulsidas was a Hindu saint and poet renowned for his devotion to Rama. He composed the Ramcharitmanas, an epic poem in the Awadhi language. | Tulsidas was a poet who retold the story of Rama in the Awadhi language.
Surdas | 16th century | India | religion, arts | poet, musician, saint | Surdas was a 16th-century blind Hindu devotional poet and singer, known for works written in praise of Krishna. | Surdas was a blind poet and singer who lived about 500 years ago. He sang about the god Krishna.
Amir Khusrau | 1253..1325 | Delhi | arts, language | poet, musician | Amir Khusrau was an Indo-Persian Sufi singer, musician and court poet of the Delhi Sultanate. | Amir Khusrau was a poet and musician at the court of the sultans of Delhi about 700 years ago.
Nizamuddin Auliya | 1238..1325-04-03 | Hazrat Nizamuddin Dargah | religion | saint | Nizamuddin Auliya was a Sufi saint. His dargah, a shrine and mosque complex, stands in the Nizamuddin area of Delhi. | Nizamuddin Auliya was a Sufi saint who lived about 700 years ago. His tomb in Delhi is a famous shrine.
Mu'in al-Din Chishti | 1143-02-01..1236-03-15 | Shrine of Mu'in al-Din Chishti | religion, movement | saint | Mu'in al-Din Chishti, known as Khawaja Gharib Nawaz, was a Persian Islamic scholar and mystic from Sistan who eventually settled in the Indian subcontinent. His shrine is at Ajmer. | Moinuddin Chishti was a Sufi holy man who came to India from Persia about 800 years ago. His tomb is at Ajmer. | Moinuddin Chishti
Alauddin Khalji | 1266..1316-01-04 | Delhi | power, war, trade | ruler | Alauddin Khalji was Sultan of Delhi, of the Khalji dynasty, from 1296 until his death in 1316. | Alauddin Khalji was a powerful sultan of Delhi from 1296 to 1316.
Muhammad bin Tughluq | 1290..1351-03-20 | Delhi | power | ruler | Muhammad bin Tughluq was a Sultan of Delhi, also known by the epithets "the Eccentric Prince" and "the Mad Sultan". | Muhammad bin Tughluq was a sultan of Delhi about 700 years ago. He was nicknamed the Eccentric Prince and the Mad Sultan.
Krishnadevaraya | 1471-01-17..1529-10-17 | Hampi | power, arts, war | ruler | Krishnadevaraya was emperor of the Vijayanagara Empire from 1509 to 1529 and the third ruler of the Tuluva dynasty. He is widely regarded as one of the greatest rulers of India. | Krishnadevaraya was the most famous emperor of Vijayanagara. He ruled from 1509 to 1529.
Madhava of Sangamagrama | 1340..1425 | Irinjalakuda | science | scientist | Madhava of Sangamagrama was a mathematician and astronomer who founded the Kerala school of astronomy and mathematics at Irinjalakuda in Kerala. | Madhava was a mathematician from Kerala who lived about 600 years ago. He started a famous school of mathematics.
Nilakantha Somayaji | 1444-06-14..1544 | Kerala | science | scientist | Nilakantha Somayaji was a mathematician and astronomer of the Kerala school of astronomy and mathematics. | Nilakantha was a mathematician and astronomer of the Kerala school.
Sankardev | 1449..1568 | Assam | religion, arts, language | saint, poet, artist, reformer | Srimanta Sankardev was a 15th–16th-century Assamese polymath: a saint-scholar, poet, playwright, dancer, actor, musician, artist and social-religious reformer. | Sankardev of Assam was a saint, poet, playwright, dancer and musician all at once. The dance called Sattriya began with his plays.
Tukaram | 17th century | Maharashtra | religion, arts | saint, poet | Tukaram, also known as Tukoba, was a Hindu Marathi saint of the Varkari tradition who lived in the 17th century. | Tukaram was a saint of Maharashtra who lived about 400 years ago. He sang his poems in Marathi.
Dnyaneshwar | 13th century | Maharashtra | religion, arts, language | saint, poet, thinker | Dnyaneshwar, also called Jnaneshwar, was a 13th-century Marathi saint, poet and philosopher. | Dnyaneshwar was a saint and poet of Maharashtra who lived about 750 years ago.
Purandara Dasa | 1470..1564 | Karnataka | arts, religion | musician, saint, poet | Purandara Dasa was a composer, singer and philosopher from present-day Karnataka. | Purandara Dasa was a singer and composer from Karnataka who lived about 500 years ago.
Tyagaraja | 1767-05-04..1847-01-06 | Thanjavur | arts, religion | musician, saint | Tyagaraja was a composer of Carnatic music, a form of Indian classical music. He lived through the reigns of four kings of the Thanjavur Maratha line but served none of them. | Tyagaraja was one of the greatest composers of south Indian classical music. Kings ruled where he lived, but he would not work for any of them.
Narsinh Mehta | 1414..1488 | Gujarat | arts, religion, language | poet, saint | Narsinh Mehta was a 15th-century poet-saint of Gujarat, honoured as the first poet, or Adi Kavi, of the Gujarati language. | Narsinh Mehta was a poet and saint of Gujarat. He is honoured as the first poet of the Gujarati language.
Malik Ambar | 1548..1626-05-14 | Ahmednagar | power, war, movement | ruler, soldier | Malik Ambar was a military leader and statesman who served as Peshwa of the Ahmadnagar Sultanate and was its de facto ruler from 1600 until his death in 1626. | Malik Ambar was a general who became the real ruler of the kingdom of Ahmadnagar in the Deccan from 1600 to 1626.
Sher Shah Suri | 1486..1545-05-22 | Sasaram | power, war, trade | ruler | Sher Shah Suri was the first Sur emperor, ruling as Sultan of Hindustan from 1540 until his death in 1545. | Sher Shah Suri took northern India from the Mughals and ruled it from 1540 to 1545.
Babur | 1483-02-14..1530-12-26 | Andijan | power, war, arts | ruler, writer | Babur, born at Andijan in the Fergana Valley, was the founder of the Mughal Empire. He was descended from Timur on his father's side and Genghis Khan on his mother's. | Babur was born in Central Asia. He invaded India and founded the Mughal Empire in 1526.
Akbar | 1542-10-15..1605 | Fatehpur Sikri | power, religion, arts | ruler | Akbar, known as Akbar the Great, was the third Mughal emperor, reigning from 1556 to 1605. He succeeded his father Humayun under a regent, Bairam Khan. | Akbar became Mughal emperor in 1556, when he was still a boy, and ruled until 1605. He is known as Akbar the Great.
Nur Jahan | 1577-05-31..1645-12-18 | India | power, women, arts | woman, ruler | Nur Jahan, born Mehr-un-Nissa, was the chief consort of the Mughal emperor Jahangir. More decisive and proactive than her husband, she is considered by some historians to have been the real power behind the throne. | Nur Jahan was the wife of the Mughal emperor Jahangir. She was so clever and bold that some historians say she was the real ruler.
Shah Jahan | 1592-01-05..1666-01-22 | Taj Mahal | power, arts | ruler, builder | Shah Jahan was the fifth Mughal emperor, from 1628 until he was deposed in 1658. His reign marked the zenith of Mughal architecture. | Shah Jahan was the Mughal emperor who built the Taj Mahal. He ruled from 1628 to 1658.
Aurangzeb | 1618-11-03..1707-03-03 | India | power, war, religion | ruler | Aurangzeb, also called Alamgir I, was the sixth Mughal emperor, reigning from 1658 until his death in 1707. Under him the empire reached its greatest extent. | Aurangzeb was the sixth Mughal emperor. He ruled for almost fifty years, and the empire was at its biggest in his time.
Dara Shikoh | 1615-03-20..1659-08-30 | India | power | ruler | Dara Shikoh was the eldest son and heir-apparent of the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan. | Dara Shikoh was the eldest son of the emperor Shah Jahan and was meant to be the next emperor.
Abul Fazl | 1551-01-14..1602-08-22 | India | language, power | writer, politician | Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak was a writer, historian and politician who served as grand vizier of the Mughal Empire. He wrote the Akbarnama and the Ain-i-Akbari. | Abul Fazl was the emperor Akbar's chief minister and historian. He wrote the story of Akbar's reign.
Shivaji | 1630-02-19..1680-04-03 | Maharashtra | power, war | ruler, soldier | Shivaji was an Indian ruler of the Bhonsle dynasty, born at the hill-fort of Shivneri. He inherited a fief from his father, who served the Sultanate of Bijapur, and founded the Maratha kingdom, being crowned in 1674. | Shivaji was born in a hill fort in 1630. He built up his own kingdom and was crowned king of the Marathas in 1674.
Bajirao I | 1700-08-18..1740-04-28 | Maharashtra | power, war | soldier, politician | Bajirao I was the seventh Peshwa of the Maratha Empire, the son of Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath. He began his military career at a young age. | Bajirao was the chief minister and general of the Marathas. He began fighting when he was very young.
Guru Gobind Singh | 1666-12-22..1708-10-07 | Anandpur Sahib | religion, war, arts | saint, soldier, poet | Guru Gobind Singh was the tenth and last human Sikh Guru: a warrior, poet and philosopher. In 1675, at the age of nine, he was formally made the leader of the Sikhs. In 1699 he founded the Khalsa. | Guru Gobind Singh became the leader of the Sikhs when he was only nine. He was a warrior and a poet, and he founded the Khalsa in 1699.
Ranjit Singh | 1801..1839 | Lahore | power, war | ruler | Ranjit Singh was the founder and first maharaja of the Sikh Empire, ruling from 1801 until his death in 1839. He fought his first battle alongside his father at the age of ten. | Maharaja Ranjit Singh founded the Sikh Empire. He fought his first battle when he was ten years old.
Tipu Sultan | 1751-12-01..1799-05-04 | Mysore | power, war, technology | ruler, soldier | Tipu Sultan, known as Sher-e-Mysore, was Sultan of Mysore from 1782 until his death in 1799. He was a pioneer of rocket artillery. | Tipu Sultan ruled Mysore from 1782 to 1799. His army used rockets in battle, which was new.
Lachit Borphukan | 1622-11-24..1672-04-25 | Guwahati | war | soldier | Lachit Borphukan was an army general who commanded the Ahom army to victory in the naval Battle of Saraighat, stopping a Mughal invasion. | Lachit Borphukan was the general of the Ahom kingdom of Assam. In 1671 he beat the Mughal navy on the Brahmaputra river.
Sawai Jai Singh | 1688-11-03..1743-09-21 | Jaipur | power, science | ruler, scientist, builder | Sawai Jai Singh II was the ruler of the Kingdom of Amber who founded the fortified city of Jaipur and made it his capital. He built the Jantar Mantar, a collection of astronomical instruments. | Sawai Jai Singh was a king who founded the city of Jaipur and built giant instruments there to study the sky.
